BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 728.0 Td (  # The Myth of the Twin Rivers) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 718.0 Td (  ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 708.0 Td (  In ancient times, two rivers flowed down from separate mountains-the River of Giving and the R) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 698.0 Td (    iver of Return. Each was fed by a different god, and between them lay a parched valley where) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 688.0 Td (     mortals struggled.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 678.0 Td (  ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 668.0 Td (  One day, a traveler named Theon stood at the River of Giving. The god spoke: "Whatever water y) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 658.0 Td (    ou pour into the valley, I shall triple." But Theon feared losing what little he had.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 648.0 Td (  ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 638.0 Td (  Across the valley, another traveler, Kara, watched. She called out: "Send your waters! I vow t) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 628.0 Td (    o return half of what flows to me, so both our vessels shall overflow.") Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 618.0 Td (  ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 608.0 Td (  Theon, trusting her word, poured his full vessel. The god tripled it, and the valley bloomed g) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 598.0 Td (    reen. Kara received abundance and, true to her word, returned half. Both travelers found the) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 588.0 Td (    mselves with more than they began.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 578.0 Td (  ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 568.0 Td (  The gods smiled and spoke as one: "When Givers trust and Receivers honor, all thirsts are quen) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 558.0 Td (    ched. But when either breaks faith-when Givers hoard or Receivers keep all-the valley remain) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 548.0 Td (    s dust.") Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 538.0 Td (  ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 528.0 Td (  The travelers became guardians of the valley, teaching others: **reciprocity transforms scarci) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 518.0 Td (    ty into abundance, but only when both give freely and return fairly.**) Tj ET
