BT /F2 8.0 Tf 48.0 688.0 Td (  Agent_5 \(Elara\)) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 678.0 Td (    # The Weaver and the River) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 668.0 Td (    ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 658.0 Td (    In ancient times, the goddess Elara discovered a silver river that flowed between two villag) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 648.0 Td (      es. The river possessed a miraculous quality: any gift cast into its waters would triple b) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 638.0 Td (      efore reaching the opposite shore.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 628.0 Td (    ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 618.0 Td (    The first village elder, hesitant and fearful, tossed only a single copper coin into the cur) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 608.0 Td (      rent. It became three on the far bank. The receiving village, grateful but cautious, retur) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 598.0 Td (      ned one coin. Both villages remained as they were-surviving, but not thriving.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 588.0 Td (    ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 578.0 Td (    Then a young weaver approached the river. She had only five threads of silk, all she owned. ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 568.0 Td (      "Trust requires courage," she whispered, casting three threads into the waters. They becam) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 558.0 Td (      e nine by the time they reached the other side.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 548.0 Td (    ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 538.0 Td (    The receiving village saw her faith. They returned six threads, keeping three for their own ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 528.0 Td (      needs. The weaver smiled-she now had eight threads instead of five.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 518.0 Td (    ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 508.0 Td (    Over many seasons, both villages learned the river's true lesson: abundance flows not from h) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 498.0 Td (      oarding, but from the sacred balance of trust and reciprocity. Those who gave generously r) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 488.0 Td (      eceived generously in return, while those who clutched their coins remained forever poor.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 478.0 Td (    ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 468.0 Td (    The silver river still flows, waiting for those brave enough to believe.) Tj ET
BT /F2 8.0 Tf 48.0 458.0 Td (  Agent_6 \(Finn\)) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 448.0 Td (    # The Myth of the Sacred Spring) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 438.0 Td (    ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 428.0 Td (    In ancient times, there was a hidden spring whose waters held a peculiar magic. When a trave) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 418.0 Td (      ler offered water from their flask to the spring, it would triple in volume, but only anot) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 408.0 Td (      her person could draw it out.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 398.0 Td (    ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 388.0 Td (    Two wanderers, the Giver and the Keeper, discovered this spring on the same day. The Giver c) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 378.0 Td (      arried a precious flask, refilled each dawn with five measures of water. The Keeper carrie) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 368.0 Td (      d an empty vessel but possessed the strength to draw from the deep spring.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 358.0 Td (    ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 348.0 Td (    At first, the Giver hesitated. "If I pour my water in, the Keeper might take it all and leav) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 338.0 Td (      e me with nothing.") Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 328.0 Td (    ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 318.0 Td (    But the spring whispered: "Trust creates abundance. Hoarding creates scarcity.") Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 308.0 Td (    ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 298.0 Td (    The Giver poured three measures into the spring. It became nine. The Keeper drew it out and ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 288.0 Td (      saw the Giver's thirst. Remembering their own thirst, the Keeper returned five measures.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 278.0 Td (    ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 268.0 Td (    Both drank deeply that day.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 258.0 Td (    ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 248.0 Td (    They returned each dawn, sometimes as Giver, sometimes as Keeper. They learned that generosi) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 238.0 Td (      ty multiplied, that fair return sustained trust, and that together they could transform sc) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 228.0 Td (      arcity into plenty.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 218.0 Td (    ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 208.0 Td (    The spring still flows, waiting for those wise enough to understand: what is shared grows, w) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 198.0 Td (      hat is hoarded diminishes, and trust rewards the trusting.) Tj ET
BT /F2 8.0 Tf 48.0 188.0 Td (  Agent_7 \(Galen\)) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 178.0 Td (    # The Myth of the Twin Rivers) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 168.0 Td (    ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 158.0 Td (    In ancient times, two rivers flowed down from separate mountains-the River of Giving and the) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 148.0 Td (       River of Return. Each was fed by a different god, and between them lay a parched valley w) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 138.0 Td (      here mortals struggled.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 128.0 Td (    ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 118.0 Td (    One day, a traveler named Theon stood at the River of Giving. The god spoke: "Whatever water) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 108.0 Td (       you pour into the valley, I shall triple." But Theon feared losing what little he had.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 98.0 Td (    ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 88.0 Td (    Across the valley, another traveler, Kara, watched. She called out: "Send your waters! I vow) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 78.0 Td (       to return half of what flows to me, so both our vessels shall overflow.") Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 68.0 Td (    ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 58.0 Td (    Theon, trusting her word, poured his full vessel. The god tripled it, and the valley bloomed) Tj ET
